{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize small-scale drug possession is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies such acts as criminal offenses. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine face a choice: accept a citation and enter a behavioral health initiative, or face misdemeanor charges carrying up to half a year in jail. The law also tightens restrictions on selling drugs near areas like playgrounds and schools, aiming to reduce visible public use and increase enforcement.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation promotes, but does not require, local jurisdictions to establish diversion pathways that steer individuals toward care rather than incarceration. While supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor responses to their capacity, critics warn it could lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions. Police are instructed to connect eligible individuals with mobile health teams within a half-hour window; if help doesn’t arrive in time, arrest becomes the default action.\n\n***\n\nOfficers must verify that the person has no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and is medically stable before initiating the alternative route. This narrow set of conditions means many may not qualify, even if they’re struggling with substance use. Chief Bob Day emphasized that law enforcement can’t delay action indefinitely — if the health team doesn’t respond within the time limit, the individual will be taken into custody.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national reckoning over how to handle addiction. In some parts of the country, people are learning to manage cravings without judgment, while others still rely on punitive measures. Whether one eats a meal or shops for groceries, the struggle for 